src/framework/contracts/README.md:1:# Semantic pass contracts
src/framework/contracts/README.md:3:This is the reusable semantic-contract layer in v5. It is independent of the CAD, scene and mechanism implementations and independent of the compiler scheduler. The integration adapter connects it to compiler acceptance policies so that both computed and cached artifacts undergo the current contract before publication.
src/framework/contracts/README.md:14:| `defineAnalysis<T,R>` | Defines a typed, versioned computation on an artifact, with parameters, effect declarations and optional checked transfer rule. |
src/framework/contracts/README.md:20:`createArtifact(ir,id,value)` validates, copies and freezes the payload and creates the same SHA-256 content/revision identities used by the existing compiler. `validateArtifact` verifies the kind, schema, value validator and both hashes. An artifact cannot pass a contract merely by claiming the expected kind or supplying an old revision next to changed data.
src/framework/contracts/README.md:24:## Writing a contract
src/framework/contracts/README.md:27:import * as C from './contracts/index.js';
src/framework/contracts/README.md:55:The geometry functions in this sketch are application implementations; the framework does not assume a universal mesh representation. Actual contracts for the included domains live under `src/contract-examples/`.
src/framework/contracts/README.md:64:- `preserves` checks that an input property holds and remains valid for an output. `mode:'check'` runs the target checker; `mode:'transfer'` requires a declared correspondence and an executable authorized transfer rule.
src/framework/contracts/README.md:69:A preservation declaration is not accepted as its own evidence. Required transfer rules are versioned parts of the analysis definition; redefining their implementation requires a new definition version.
src/framework/contracts/README.md:78:| `hasAnalysis(definition,artifact,params?)` | Check current reusable availability, including transitive dependencies and explicit invalidations. |
src/framework/contracts/README.md:80:| `checkInvariant(invariant,namedInputs)` | Query a relational invariant independently; returns checked evidence and records its actual typed inputs. |
src/framework/contracts/README.md:83:| `preserve(analysis,from,to,correspondence,params?)` | Transfer a valid existing result only after the registered transfer checker approves this exact correspondence. |
src/framework/contracts/README.md:84:| `invalidate(analysisOrProperty,artifact,reason?)` | Invalidate information and advance its epoch; dependent information becomes unavailable, including a checker still computing when invalidation occurs. |
src/framework/contracts/README.md:90:Analyses may depend on analyses, properties and versioned resources. These dependencies are retained as exact record IDs and resource revisions. A change to a dependency, definition version, artifact revision or invalidation epoch prevents stale reuse. Cycles across analyses and properties are diagnosed. Analysis/fact identities are deterministic for equivalent computations, so concurrent verification of the same immutable source does not create false conflicts.
src/framework/contracts/README.md:106:All asynchronous context requests must be awaited. Pending requests are observed, drained and rejected at lifecycle boundaries; they cannot silently finish after output acceptance. Contexts close after their owner returns. Rolling back a transaction immediately closes every descendant context, so pending work cannot publish late analysis or fact records. Every asynchronous query captures artifact revisions, parameters, certificates and correspondences before awaiting caller code. Fact-set/explanation introspection can observe absent information, so its computations and dependants conservatively disable caching rather than pretending an incomplete dependency list captures absence.
src/framework/contracts/README.md:108:## Evidence and transfer
src/framework/contracts/README.md:110:Every property fact identifies its exact artifact ID, revision, kind and schema version, the property and checker versions, the supporting analysis, dependencies, current validity and assurance:
src/framework/contracts/README.md:114:- `transferred`: an existing valid result was carried to another revision after an authorized transfer checker verified the explicit correspondence.
src/framework/contracts/README.md:119:A correspondence contains `id`, exact `from` and `to` artifact references, `relation`, optional entity groups and application evidence. It is small relationship data, not the generic IR editing framework proposed separately. Transfer checks validate exact revisions and their semantic justification. Same-artifact preservation reuses the existing analysis without introducing a self-dependency. Source invalidation also invalidates transferred results.
src/framework/contracts/README.md:128:const tx = await manager.beginTransformation(contract, namedInputs, {
src/framework/contracts/README.md:137:`verifyTransformation(before,after,contract,correspondences?,options?)` is a convenience for an already computed lowering. It commits by default; `deferCommit:true` returns a prepared transaction for a host acceptance gate.
src/framework/contracts/README.md:145:A cached artifact is still checked under the current contract. The body has its own tracked dependency frame; pre/post checks do not masquerade as computational dependencies.
src/framework/contracts/README.md:147:`execution.bodyDependencies` and `bodyCacheable` describe what the previous kernel used. Before an external compiler reuses that kernel's artifact, it calls `manager.canReuseDependencies(previous.bodyDependencies)`. If reuse is allowed, `tx.importBodyDependencies(...)` validates and imports those dependencies, resource compare-and-swap stamps, permissions and evidence links. Then current guarantees run normally. Importing the complete old manifest on every warm run prevents a second cache hit from dropping body-only dependencies.
src/framework/contracts/README.md:153:`ResourcePort` exposes synchronous snapshot reads and an atomic synchronous commit of staged JSON writes against expected original revisions. `MemoryResources` is the included reference adapter. The port contract requires all-or-nothing commit: it validates all names/values and expected revisions before applying any write. Async external systems can be adapted by preparing a stable snapshot and using an appropriately transactional host commit boundary; they must not claim atomicity they cannot provide. Native async adapter methods and thenable commit results are rejected; rejected asynchronous adapter work is observed but cannot be undone by the manager. The host adapter remains responsible for its atomicity guarantee.
src/framework/contracts/README.md:155:Resource access is checked transitively. An analysis's declared read does not grant its caller permission; the enclosing transformation must declare the effect too. Cached analysis reuse checks the same permissions and dependency revisions. Missing-resource reads record a `missing` revision before throwing, so a caught fallback is invalidated if the resource later appears.
src/framework/contracts/README.md:157:Writes are staged and visible to subsequent reads in the same transaction. Preconditions can read the original resource state, while postconditions see staged revisions. Commit checks the physical original revisions for conflicts and validates post-write dependencies against the proposed final view before installing writes and facts. A rejected postcondition publishes neither the proposed resource state nor its facts.
src/framework/contracts/README.md:159:These rules enforce use of the explicit effect port. They do not sandbox arbitrary JavaScript: an application implementation that directly changes global variables, files or network state outside the port violates the host contract and cannot be made pure by declaration. `cache:'pure'` is not a security boundary. The same qualification applies to caller-supplied validators and transfer/certificate verifiers; their versions and behavior remain an application responsibility.
src/framework/contracts/README.md:163:This module implements semantic pass contracts and analysis management. It does not introduce a universal AST, generic rewrite matcher, transaction-based IR editor, e-graph, static effect inference, persistent cache backend or formal proof engine. Those are separate architectural layers.
src/framework/ir/README.md:3:This package implements the third layer independently of the scheduler, semantic contracts and domain implementations. Public exports are in `index.ts`. Domain adapters retain their native representations and expose a finite logical projection for shared algorithms.
src/framework/ir/README.md:7:`defineAdapter(definition)` captures versioned adapter metadata and callbacks from a plain record with own callback properties. Wrap class methods explicitly when adapting an existing class implementation. The mandatory methods are `entities`, `resolve`, `relations` and `describe`; `relationKinds` assigns each named edge kind to containment, dependency, binding, adjacency or provenance. Enumeration and resolution must agree. Duplicate IDs, invalid edge categories, missing endpoints and malformed roots are rejected.
src/framework/ir/README.md:17:`entities`, `resolve`, `describe`, `relations`, `operation`, `find`, `graph`, `serialize` and `visualize` inspect the bound representation. `children`, `parents` and `ancestors` follow containment only. `successors` and `predecessors` follow explicitly named edge kinds. `dependencies` defaults to transitive dependency and binding edges; use `{transitive:false}` for one step.
src/framework/ir/README.md:21:`subgraph(selection)` returns the induced logical subgraph. Select `closure:'dependencies'` or `closure:'connected'` for a closure, and optionally restrict its relation kinds and node budget. This is a view projection, not automatically a valid standalone native artifact.
src/framework/ir/README.md:35:| `redirectReferences(from,to,options?)` | Redirect incoming binding/dependency edges by default; other kinds/directions must be explicit |
src/framework/ir/README.md:52:`makeCorrespondence`, `validateCorrespondence`, `composeCorrespondence`, `invertCorrespondence` and `traceEntity` work independently of a particular native format. A grouped correspondence denotes the Cartesian relation between its source and target IDs. Empty endpoints represent additions or removals. Source and target universes are explicit and fully accounted for.
src/framework/ir/README.md:56:Correspondence records association, not semantic equivalence. A domain checker must justify any preservation or analysis transfer that uses it.
src/framework/ir/README.md:60:See [Patterns and rewrite schedules](PATTERNS-AND-REWRITES.md) for all builders, matching evidence, registries, seven schedules, order selection, budgets, scoped edit authority and failure statuses. Pure pattern/guard callbacks receive a frozen `IRReader`; rewrite callbacks receive a mutation-only `RewriteEdit`. The scheduler retains prepare/commit authority and seals callback access before semantic acceptance.
